message.content:
Below is a quick “cook‑book” for using the `areDeclarationFlagsIdentical` helper that ships with the `functions` namespace.  
It will let you compare two **TypeScript declaration objects** (e.g. a parameter vs. a variable declaration) and tell you whether their *declaration flags* match, taking into account the usual suspects:

| Flag | What it means |
|------|---------------|
| `optional` | `?` on a parameter or `?` on a property |
| `private` / `protected` / `public` | Access modifier |
| `async` | `async` keyword on a function/parameter |
| `abstract` | `abstract` keyword on a class member |
| `readonly` | `readonly` keyword on a property |
| `static` | `static` keyword on a class member |

---

## 1. What the function expects

```ts
// In JavaScript/TypeScript syntax
const result = functions.areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({
    left:  JSON.stringify(parameterObject),
    right: JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject)
});
```

* `left`  – a **stringified** representation of the first declaration object (the parameter).  
* `right` – a **stringified** representation of the second declaration object (the variable).

The function will parse those strings back into objects internally, walk through the flag properties, and return a boolean (`true` if the flags match, `false` otherwise).

---

## 2. Example

Suppose you have the following two declaration objects:

```ts
// Parameter declaration
const parameterObject = {
    kind: "Parameter",
    name: "x",
    type: "number",
    optional: false,
    modifiers: ["public"]   // (for a constructor parameter)
};

// Variable declaration
const variableDeclarationObject = {
    kind: "Variable",
    name: "x",
    type: "number",
    optional: false,
    modifiers: ["public"]
};
```

You can compare them like this:

```ts
const leftStr  = JSON.stringify(parameterObject);
const rightStr = JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject);

const identical = functions.areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({ left: leftStr, right: rightStr });

console.log(identical); // → true
```

If you change one of the flags (e.g., make the variable `readonly`), the function will return `false`.

---

## 3. What to do if you don’t have the objects yet

If you’re working directly in a TypeScript compiler API context, you can extract the relevant flags from the AST nodes and build plain objects that mirror the shape above. For example:

```ts
import { ts } from "typescript";

function extractFlags(node: ts.Node): any {
    const flags: any = {};

    // Optional
    flags.optional = !!(node as any).questionToken;

    // Modifiers
    const mods = ts.getCombinedModifierFlags(node as any);
    flags.modifiers = [];
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Private) flags.modifiers.push("private");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Protected) flags.modifiers.push("protected");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Public) flags.modifiers.push("public");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Static) flags.modifiers.push("static");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Readonly) flags.modifiers.push("readonly");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Abstract) flags.modifiers.push("abstract");
    if (mods & ts.ModifierFlags.Async) flags.modifiers.push("async");

    return flags;
}
```

Then feed the resulting objects into the comparison function.

---

## 4. Quick checklist before calling

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✅ | Convert both declaration objects to JSON strings (`JSON.stringify`). |
| ✅ | Ensure the objects contain the same set of flag keys (`optional`, `modifiers`, etc.). |
| ✅ | Call `functions.areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({ left, right })`. |
| ✅ | Inspect the boolean result. |

---

### TL;DR

```ts
const identical = functions.areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({
    left:  JSON.stringify(parameterObject),
    right: JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject)
});

if (identical) {
    console.log("The declaration flags match!");
} else {
    console.log("The declaration flags differ.");
}
```

Feel free to paste your actual `parameterObject` and `variableDeclarationObject` (or the JSON strings) here, and I can run the comparison for you!
